{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790888590,"nonce":"o3R9gWe/tl0fy/zCRi0X4o+nOK8=","title":"An Investigator cannot propose a Remedy, and no approved change can be executed","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0054 §1, §2, §5, §6. Remedy lifecycle `proposed → approved → executed | failed`, or `declined`, or `expired`, each by a named actor; proposable only when a configured write Tool can execute it; approval in oto's UI shows the exact command first; execution is a separate step through the ToolServer with the approved arguments; never retried automatically; every transition goes outbound to the declared Incident.\n\n**Why it matters.** It is the write path. **No Remedy may execute before risk controls exist**, so this slice carries the two controls that do not need operator rules: anything the risk rules cannot parse (`sh -c`, pipes) needs double approval, and double approval means **two different grant holders**. Until operator-written risk rules exist (the follow-up ticket), there are no rules, so **every Remedy is double approval** — the choice made here instead of shipping Remedies disabled.\n\n**Where.** Remedy table; write-Tool binding on ToolServers; approval UI; executor job; outbound reasons in `aa6d18b`'s vocabulary.\n\n**Scope.** large — split at implementation if needed into proposal+approval and execution.\n\n**Done when.** A Remedy runs only after approval by two different holders of the grant on its ToolServer; the same user approving twice counts once; the Investigator never holds the write Tool; a failed Remedy stays failed; approval expires after the operator-set time; each transition appears on the external incident as a fact; tests cover every transition and the two-approver rule.\n\n**Blocked by.** `2e9a086`, `47f67c8`, `aa6d18b`\n\n**Mode.** AFK — can be implemented and merged without a human decision. Source: ADR 0052/0053/0054 design session, 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}